*Modena Engine* | V-$CHIPVR ($CHIPCN)

*The Model*

Chippy does not run your program by walking the syntax tree, which is slow. It compiles the program to bytecode first, and a virtual machine then executes that bytecode. The engine behind this is called "Modena", it contains a bytecode compiler, serializer, virtual machine and disassembler. The VM is a stack machine.

*Architecture*

The lexer turns the text into tokens, the parser turns the tokens into a syntax tree, the compiler turns that tree into a chunk of bytecode, and the virtual machine runs the chunk. All of this happens automatically in memory when you run a program.

'''
                 chippy <program>
                        |
          +-------------+-------------+
          |                           |
        source                     bytecode
          |                           |
+---------+---------+                 |
|      Lexer        |                 |
|      Parser       |                 |
|      Compiler     |                 |
+---------+---------+                 |
          |                           |
          +-------------+-------------+
                        |
          +-------------+-------------+
          |           Chunk           |
          |                           |
          |  code                     |
          |  constants                |
          |  names                    |
          |  functions                |
          |  local slots              |
          |  source spans             |
          +-------------+-------------+
                        |
          +-------------+-------------+
          |         Modena VM         |
          |                           |
          |  value stack              |
          |  call frames              |
          |  local slots              |
          |  global slots             |
          +-------------+-------------+
                        |
          +-------------+-------------+
          |Values, Builtins, Optionals|
          +---------------------------+
'''

*Source or Bytecode*

A program can be given to the engine as source code or as compiled bytecode. The engine can tell them apart automatically, so ''chippy <program>'' and e.g. ''load(filename)'' can accept either form and there is nothing extra to do.

*Disassembling*

''chippy disasm <file>'' outputs the instructions the compiler produced, along with the constants. It accepts source files and compiled files. A source file is compiled in memory first, so its listing also shows the source line that each instruction came from. A compiled file carries no positions at all, therefore it is not showing any line numbers.

For example, see this simple ''"Hello World!"'' program:

'''
#!/usr/bin/chippy

fwrite(pack("Hello World!"), 1);
'''

which compiles to:

'''
== helloworld.chp ==
0000  OpGetGlobal      0 (fwrite) ; line 3
0005  OpGetGlobal      1 (pack)
0010  OpConstant       0 ("Hello World!")
0015  OpCall           1
0020  OpConstant       1 (1)
0025  OpCall           2
'''

This is practical for example when you do not know what a specific program is doing and want to audit it, or something in your program is going wrong and the compiler is suspected of producing bad instructions.

